I'll analyze both sets of numbers.

**Q1: 178, 200, 146, 174, 124, 166, 132, 134, 140, 182**

- All are even numbers (divisible by 2)
- Range: 124 to 200 (span of 76)
- Sum: 178+200+146+174+124+166+132+134+140+182 = 1576
- Mean: 157.6
- All are between 120 and 200
- They are all composite numbers (not prime)
- Several are close to multiples of 10 or 20 (140, 166, 174, 182, 200)
- Differences between consecutive sorted values: 124, 132, 134, 140, 146, 166, 174, 178, 182, 200 — gaps of 8, 2, 6, 6, 20, 8, 4, 4, 18
- No obvious arithmetic or geometric pattern

**Q2: 103, 101, 197, 151, 127, 137, 179, 199, 191, 109**

- All are odd numbers
- All are prime numbers (each is only divisible by 1 and itself)
- Range: 101 to 199 (span of 98)
- Sum: 103+101+197+151+127+137+179+199+191+109 = 1494
- Mean: 149.4
- All are between 100 and 200
- They are all primes in the 100–200 range
- Sorted: 101, 103, 109, 127, 137, 151, 179, 191, 197, 199
- Gaps: 2, 6, 18, 10, 14, 28, 12, 6, 2 — no simple pattern
- Notably, 101 and 103 are twin primes, as are 197 and 199

**Key contrast:**  
- Set 1: all even, composite numbers  
- Set 2: all odd, prime numbers  
Both sets are in the 100–200 range, but they represent opposite parity and primality properties. [end of text]


